## Proof

**Answer-leak check:** The problem statement contains only the integral to compute. No solution, hint, or answer value is embedded. Proceeding.

**Goal:** Compute
$$I = \iint_{\mathbb{R}^2} e^{-(|2x-y|+|2y-x|)}\,dx\,dy.$$

### Step 1. Linear change of variables

Set
$$u = 2x - y, \qquad v = 2y - x.$$

The Jacobian matrix of the map $(x,y)\mapsto (u,v)$ is
$$J = \begin{pmatrix} \partial u/\partial x & \partial u/\partial y \\ \partial v/\partial x & \partial v/\partial y \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 & -1 \\ -1 & 2 \end{pmatrix},$$
with determinant
$$\det J = (2)(2) - (-1)(-1) = 4 - 1 = 3 \neq 0.$$

Hence the map is a bijection $\mathbb{R}^2 \to \mathbb{R}^2$, and
$$du\,dv = |\det J|\,dx\,dy = 3\,dx\,dy \quad\Longrightarrow\quad dx\,dy = \tfrac{1}{3}\,du\,dv.$$

### Step 2. Rewrite the integral

Under this substitution the exponent becomes $|u| + |v|$, so
$$I = \frac{1}{3}\iint_{\mathbb{R}^2} e^{-(|u|+|v|)}\,du\,dv = \frac{1}{3}\left(\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-|u|}\,du\right)\!\left(\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-|v|}\,dv\right).$$

### Step 3. Evaluate the one-dimensional factor

By symmetry of $e^{-|t|}$ about $0$,
$$\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} e^{-|t|}\,dt = 2\int_{0}^{\infty} e^{-t}\,dt = 2\cdot 1 = 2.$$

### Step 4. Combine

$$I = \frac{1}{3}\cdot 2 \cdot 2 = \frac{4}{3}.$$

### Conclusion

$$\boxed{\iint_{\mathbb{R}^2} e^{-(|2x-y|+|2y-x|)}\,dx\,dy = \frac{4}{3}}.$$

